In 2009, the Government of India named the Ganges river dolphin the national aquatic animal. Scientists call it an indicator [[species|species]]. If the dolphins are doing well, the river is probably healthy too, with enough water, fish and clean stretches.

Bihar has the Vikramshila Gangetic Dolphin Sanctuary, a stretch of about 60 kilometres of the Ganga near Bhagalpur, set up in 1991. Uttar Pradesh had the largest number of dolphins in the 2025 survey, followed by Bihar.

{{story:ganga-journey}}

On the eastern edge of [[@kolkata|Kolkata]] lies a maze of ponds, fields and channels called the East Kolkata Wetlands. Every day, a large share of the city's sewage flows into them. Sunlight, [[bacteria|bacteria]] and tiny [[algae|algae]] break the waste down in the shallow ponds. Fish farmers then raise fish in the cleaner water, and vegetables grow on its banks.

Local people worked out this system over many decades, long before engineers studied it. In 2002 the wetlands were named a Ramsar site, a wetland of international importance. Building a treatment plant to do the same job would cost a fortune. The marsh does it with sunshine, patience and fish. Some of that fish ends up in the city's markets, so in a sense Kolkata eats what its wetlands have cleaned.

