When Yellowstone became the world's first [[@national-parks|national park]] in 1872, wolves were seen as pests that killed cattle and deer. Rangers and hunters shot, trapped and poisoned them. By about 1926, the last known pack in the park had been killed.

For almost seventy years, Yellowstone had no wolves. Scientists slowly realised that the park, famous for its geysers such as [[@old-faithful|Old Faithful]], was missing one of its main hunters. After years of public argument, including strong objections from ranchers, the government agreed to bring wolves back.

