Box cookers are cheap and safe, and food rarely burns, so you can leave it to cook while you do other things. Panel cookers are even simpler: shiny folded panels, often of card and foil, bounce sunlight onto a dark pot inside a clear plastic bag.

Parabolic cookers use a large curved dish of mirrors, shaped like [[@satellite-tv|a satellite dish]], to focus sunlight onto one spot. They get very hot, very fast, and can fry food. They must be handled carefully, because the bright spot can burn skin and eyes.

