Home charging uses AC, the same kind of [[@electricity-made|electricity]] that comes out of the wall. A box inside the car converts it into DC, the kind a battery stores. That converter is small, which limits how fast home charging can go.

Fast chargers do the converting inside the big roadside machine and feed DC straight into the battery. They need thick, often cooled cables, and a supply strong enough to run several houses. In the United States, many car makers are moving to the same plug that Tesla designed. India uses a mix of standards, which is why charging stations often have more than one type of plug.

{{story:renewable-energy}}

Some inventions solve a problem people don't actually feel. The Segway, launched in 2001, was a two-wheeled scooter that balanced itself with gyroscopes and [[sensor|sensors]]. Its makers hinted it would change how cities moved. It worked, but it cost about as much as a small motorbike, and people weren't sure where to ride it. Segway stopped making it in 2020.

In 2016, an American company launched a $700 machine, later cut to $400, that squeezed juice out of sealed packets. Reporters then showed you could squeeze the packets just as well by hand. The company shut down within about a year and a half.

