Accounts of Musa's caravan describe thousands of people, from soldiers and officials to servants. Camels carried loads of gold. Some numbers in old books, like 60,000 people, are probably exaggerated, but everyone agrees it was huge.

In Cairo, the writer al-Umari spoke to people who had met Musa. They said he handed out gold so freely that the metal lost value in the city's markets for about 12 years. By the end of his journey, Musa had run short and borrowed money to get home. The story spread across the Mediterranean.

{{story:sahara}}

Most of the islands are covered in thick [[tropical|tropical]] forest, with giant trees, orchids and [[@mangroves|mangroves]] along the coasts. Under the water, [[@coral-reefs|coral reefs]] ring many islands. Divers see parrotfish, [[@manta-rays|manta rays]], turtles and, rarely, the dugong, a gentle sea [[mammal|mammal]] that grazes on seagrass. The dugong is the territory's official animal.

{{story:coral-reefs}}

Saltwater [[@croc-ancient|crocodiles]] live in the creeks and mangroves. Many birds, snakes and plants on the islands are found nowhere else, because they [[evolve|evolved]] separately from the mainland.

[[@sound-travel|Sound]] is a wave of pushes travelling through air at about 343 metres a second. When it hits a hard, smooth surface, it bounces back as an [[@echoes|echo]]. In a normal room, the bounces come so fast that they blur together. Gol Gumbaz is so big that each bounce takes long enough for your ear to hear separately.

The dome's curve also gathers the sound and sends it back down towards the centre, like a giant bowl. Plaster and stone absorb very little, so each bounce is only a bit weaker than the last. That is why one clap can come back seven times, or even more in a quiet hall.

{{story:echoes}}

In 1821, a former French army officer named Charles Barbier visited the school. He had invented "night writing", a code of raised dots that soldiers could read by touch without lighting a lamp, which might give away their position. Barbier's system used cells of up to 12 dots, and its patterns stood for sounds, not letters. It was too big for a fingertip to feel in one go.

Young Louis worked on the idea for three years. By 1824, aged 15, he had shrunk the cell to six dots, small enough to sit under one fingertip, and matched the patterns to letters. He published his system in 1829 and later added patterns for music and maths.

{{story:skin}}

Fresh food spoils quickly in heat. The answer is the cold chain, which keeps food chilled at every step, from cold stores near farms to refrigerated trucks to shop [[@refrigerator|refrigerators]].

India produces huge amounts of fruit, vegetables and milk, but cold storage does not reach every village. A tomato picked in the heat and carried on an open truck may bruise or rot before it is sold. Better cold chains are one of the simplest ways to cut [[@food-waste|food waste]].

{{story:green-revolution}}

[[archaeologist|Archaeologists]] digging at Yinxu, the old capital of the Shang [[dynasty|dynasty]] in central China, found bronze chopsticks dating to about 1200 BCE. Bronze would have been expensive, so most people probably used bamboo or wood, which rotted away long ago.

For centuries, the Chinese ate many grains, such as millet, with spoons. Chopsticks became the main eating tool around the time of the Han dynasty, about 2,000 years ago, as [[@noodles-pasta|noodles]] and dumplings grew popular. By around the year 1400, they were the normal way to eat in China.

